{"records":[{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8f9mbc6hwt","title":"Data for: Understanding the Influence of Maternal Hepatitis B E Antibodies Transfer in Neonates: Analyzing Antibodies, myeloid-derived suppressor cells (MDSCs), and their Interplay in the Context of Chronic Hepatitis B and Chronic Hepatitis B-Delta Coi...","description":"This data set consists of 87 distinct samples extracted from the case report, along with three existing tests on the mother of the individual mentioned in the report. All these tests confirm the negative Hepatitis Antibodies status of the mother, indicating that there was no transfer of maternal Hepatitis B E antibodies from the mother to the individual at the time of birth in the reported case.\nIn order to elucidate the positivity of Hepatitis B E Antigen in presented case, I sought to correlate the well-known history of no hepatitis B infection within the family, particularly focusing on the absent immunological makeup transferred from mother. In the case presented, the history of no hepatitis B and the absence of vaccination in the mother correlates with the absence of maternal transfer of Hepatitis B antibodies, respectively with the absence of Hepatitis B E Antibodies at birth. Furthermore, the available data sets provide confirmation of the absence of inflammation resulting from non-subsequent exposure to other hepatitis viruses. This supports the hypothesis that the positivity and persistence of Hepatitis B E Antigen are maintained through various processes. The presence of Hepatitis B E Antigen is associated with the absence of maternal Hepatitis B E antibodies transfer from mother, while the persistence of Hepatitis B E Antigen antigen is maintained by the absence of superinfection with different hepatitis viruses in the presented case. Moreover, there is a strong likelihood that the same process is involved in the positivity of Hepatitis B E in neonates exposed to hepatitis B who were born from HBe antigen-positive mothers. As an alternative to the hypothesis generated, neonates that are exposed to hepatitis B and are born from mothers with positive IgG HBc and HBe antibodies and negative HBs antibodies, have positive Hepatitis B E antibodies (HBe AB) at birth, as a result of acquired immunity by placental transfer of maternal IgG antibodies. This transfer of antibodies is essential for controlling the initial diseases and results in Hepatitis B E antigen neutralization, providing partial defense by secondary antibodies immune response. Therefore, these findings elucidate a novel mechanism responsible for HBe Antigen positivity and HBe antigen neutralization in neonates.\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-08T17:09:15.273Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8f9mbc6hwt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8f9mbc6hwt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cosmin","last_name":"Oprea","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"This analysis was conducted independently, without any grant or funding agency, commercial or non-profit sectors. ","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Immunology","Hepatitis D Virus","Hepatitis B Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122707,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":108,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122707,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122215,"restricted":60,"embargoed":432},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1949},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":606},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":395},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":384},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":348},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":348},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":315},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":225}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120179},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1865},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":160},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":123},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":67},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":27},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":406},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":403},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":347},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":344},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":330}]}}